{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789485425,"nonce":"1FFb8fGTr4QCP++H+9kDx4zvLH8=","title":"A dict literal with distinct string keys is built by search","message":"`{\"id\": i, \"group\": g, \"score\": s, \"flag\": f}` built a list of the eight values, then `zb_dict_from_pairs` made an empty dict and inserted each pair with a search of the pairs before it (`zb_any_eq` on every earlier key, six string compares for four keys), growing the dict's storage two slots at a time; then the pairs list was released.\n\nWhen every key is a string literal and no two are equal, the frontend now lays the dict's storage out itself (the index slot, then the pairs) and `zb_dict_from_distinct` takes it as the dict, giving it a table only past the small-dict size. No search, no second list, no growth.\n\nMeasured on the M1 Pro: building 600 k four-key rows 125 to 63 ms; bench_records exec 200 to 130 ms, of which 37 ms are its two collections.\n\nNot done: keys that are distinct literals of other types (ints, None, bools), and the settled-shape representation (24cef98), which would go further by keeping a row as a struct.","files":null}]}